1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:09,468
This is Maison Aurelia, a hotel management system for small hotels. Let’s look at the problems they face, how the app solves each one, and what’s not there yet.

2
00:00:09,668 --> 00:00:18,165
Most small hotels run on spreadsheets, a separate billing tool, WhatsApp groups, and a website that can’t take bookings. That causes four problems.

3
00:00:18,365 --> 00:00:23,725
One: double bookings. The website and the front desk can both sell the last room.

4
00:00:23,925 --> 00:00:30,441
Two: the rules live in people’s heads. Deposits, refunds and tax depend on who’s on shift.

5
00:00:30,641 --> 00:00:36,595
Three: there’s no single record of a stay, so checkout is slow and disputes are hard to settle.

6
00:00:36,795 --> 00:00:43,496
And four: no insight or accountability. Nobody sees revenue at a glance, or knows who changed what.

7
00:00:43,696 --> 00:00:49,529
And guests expect to do all of this online, without phoning the hotel. Let’s see how the app handles it.

8
00:00:49,729 --> 00:00:56,123
Here’s the guest website. The booking bar checks live availability, so the hotel’s own site can finally take bookings.

9
00:00:56,323 --> 00:01:04,214
Nothing here is hard-coded. Prices and rooms come from MySQL, and ratings come from guest reviews in Neo4j.

10
00:01:04,414 --> 00:01:11,214
And this cancellation policy is the same setting the database uses for refunds, so what the site promises is what the hotel does.

11
00:01:11,414 --> 00:01:16,580
Pick your dates, and every room shows real availability for those nights, down to how many are left.

12
00:01:16,780 --> 00:01:20,769
Prices show the full stay and the tax, straight from the hotel’s settings.

13
00:01:20,969 --> 00:01:27,400
Guests can book up to five rooms at once. It’s one transaction, so either every room is reserved, or none is.

14
00:01:27,600 --> 00:01:34,290
Reviews and recommendations come from the Neo4j graph. If it’s ever down, the site keeps working without them.

15
00:01:34,490 --> 00:01:41,582
Let’s book one for real. Signed in as a guest, the dates are set, and the price, tax and total are worked out instantly.

16
00:01:41,782 --> 00:01:46,023
Reserving holds the room straight away, and asks for a deposit to confirm it.

17
00:01:46,223 --> 00:01:54,095
The payment is simulated, and once the deposit lands, a database trigger confirms the booking. That’s the whole journey, with no phone call.

18
00:01:54,295 --> 00:02:01,973
Signed in as a guest, here’s the loyalty tier. Points are added automatically at check-out, so everyone earns the same way.

19
00:02:02,173 --> 00:02:11,603
Each booking shows the room, services and balance in one place. Guests can pay, order services, get an invoice, or cancel, without calling the desk.

20
00:02:11,803 --> 00:02:19,316
One gap: payments are simulated. There’s no real payment gateway yet, but Razorpay or Stripe can be plugged into one place later.

21
00:02:19,516 --> 00:02:25,406
Changing a booking re-prices it live, re-checks availability, and refunds any overpayment automatically.

22
00:02:25,606 --> 00:02:33,109
Now the front desk. Every reservation, from the website or the desk, lives in one list with its status and balance.

23
00:02:33,309 --> 00:02:37,992
Each row only offers actions that are allowed. You can’t check a guest out while they still owe money.

24
00:02:38,192 --> 00:02:44,567
When the desk books a room, the database locks it and re-checks the dates, so the same room can never be sold twice.

25
00:02:44,767 --> 00:02:45,890
And that’s tested.

26
00:02:46,090 --> 00:02:52,694
Two requests for the last room, at the same moment. Exactly one wins. All twenty tests pass.

27
00:02:52,894 --> 00:02:58,460
Housekeeping moves off WhatsApp. Check-out creates a cleaning task, and finishing it frees the room.

28
00:02:58,660 --> 00:03:04,683
Managers get the big picture: arrivals, occupancy, revenue and what’s still owed, all live.

29
00:03:04,883 --> 00:03:08,954
Here’s the occupancy forecast, and the status of every room right now.

30
00:03:09,154 --> 00:03:16,594
Today’s arrivals have one-click check-in. The activity feed refreshes every twelve seconds; instant real-time updates aren’t there yet.

31
00:03:16,794 --> 00:03:21,993
For deeper questions, sixteen SQL reports run live, with CSV export.

32
00:03:22,193 --> 00:03:32,324
And here’s the fix for rules in people’s heads. Tax, deposit and cancellation rules live in one place. Change a number here, and the very next booking follows it.

33
00:03:32,524 --> 00:03:39,676
Every role sees only what it needs. Permissions live in the database, so a receptionist can’t open settings or salaries.

34
00:03:39,876 --> 00:03:48,801
And the audit log. Here’s a booking a guest just made, recorded automatically with who did it. Every change and payment is logged the same way.

35
00:03:49,001 --> 00:03:53,740
Its confirmation email is waiting here, but it isn’t sent until email settings are added.

36
00:03:53,940 --> 00:03:57,920
It all works on a phone. There’s no native app yet, but the website covers it.

37
00:03:58,120 --> 00:03:59,982
So, what’s not there yet?

38
00:04:00,182 --> 00:04:03,770
No real payment gateway, and no link to sites like Booking.com.

39
00:04:03,970 --> 00:04:06,708
Email needs setting up, and the live feed isn’t instant.

40
00:04:06,908 --> 00:04:11,251
It’s one hotel, one currency, and it still needs browser and load testing.

41
00:04:11,451 --> 00:04:19,319
To sum up: no double bookings, rules in one place, one record per stay, a full audit trail, and guests who can serve themselves.

42
00:04:19,519 --> 00:04:25,607
Next up: real payments, automated browser tests, cloud photo storage, and dynamic pricing.

43
00:04:25,807 --> 00:04:29,441
That’s Maison Aurelia. Thanks for watching.
